    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper discusses the development of faults and folds in Elk Hills, CA, and considers the possible contribution of inelastic deformation and mechanical stratigraphy to the evolution of these structures. Elk Hills is a well-studied oil field that consists of a series of thrust faults and anticlines in sedimentary rocks that date to the Miocene. The most famous of the stratigraphic units in Elk Hills is the Monterey Formation. Fiore et al. (2007) present a mechanical model assuming linear elasticity in order to reconstruct the evolution of Elk Hills. Because the Monterey Formation shows evidence of inelastic deformation, we suggest that viscoplastic constitutive laws may better represent the deformational style in this area. We will test this hypothesis by using finite element analysis and an appropriate modeling program that assumes viscoplastic constitutive laws to reconstruct the two- dimensional evolution of Elk Hills. In addition, the model will take into account the differences in mechanical properties between the different stratigraphic packages. This modeling experiment may improve our understanding of inelastic deformation and mechanical stratigraphy, particularly in the context of fold and thrust belt evolution, and with implications for hydrocarbon migration and emplacement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
